      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) in a rectangular prism with dimensions 10-inches by 20-inches by 10-inches, we can use the 3-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. \n\nFirst, let's label the dimensions of the rectangular prism for clarity:\n- Length \\( l = 20 \\) inches\n- Width \\( w = 10 \\) inches\n- Height \\( h = 10 \\) inches\n\nThe diagonal \\( d \\) of a rectangular prism can be found using the formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given dimensions into the formula, we get:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate the squares of each dimension:\n\\[\n20^2 = 400\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nNext, sum these squares:\n\\[\n400 + 100 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root of the sum to find the length of the diagonal:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\), we look for the largest perfect square that is a factor of 600:\n\\[\n600 = 4 \\times 150\n\\]\n\\[\n4 = 2^2\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{4 \\times 150} = \\sqrt{4} \\times \\sqrt{150} = 2\\sqrt{150}\n\\]\n\nWe can further simplify \\(\\sqrt{150}\\) by factoring it into its prime factors:\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{150} = \\sqrt{2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2} = \\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{3} \\times 5 = 5\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{150} = 2 \\times 5\\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
